ydata-profiling (formerly pandas-profiling)Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) in a single line of code.ydata-profiling delivers an extended analysis of a DataFrame without writing custom code for every chart and statistic. It generates an interactive HTML report containing descriptive statistics, correlation matrices, quantile statistics, and detailed distribution plots.🚀 Key FeaturesType Inference: Automatically detects numerical, categorical, and boolean features.Essentials: Type, unique values, missing values, most frequent values, variable size.Quantile Statistics: Minimum value, Q1, median, Q3, maximum, range, interquartile range.Descriptive Statistics: Mean, mode, standard deviation, sum, median absolute deviation, coefficient of variation, kurtosis, skewness.Histograms: Visualization of distributions for every variable.Correlations: Spearman, Pearson, Kendall, Cramér's V, and Phik correlation matrices with heatmaps.Missing Values: Matrix, Count, Heatmap, and Dendrogram of missing data patterns.Text Analysis: Learn about categories (Uppercase, Space), Script (Latin, Cyrillic), and Blocks (ASCII) for text data.📦 InstallationYou can install ydata-profiling using pip or conda.Using pippip install ydata-profiling
Using condaconda install -c conda-forge ydata-profiling
> Note: The package was previously named pandas-profiling. If you are using an older version, please upgrade to ydata-profiling for the latest features.💻 Usage1. Basic Usage (Generate & Save Report)The most common use case is to load a dataset and save the report as an HTML file.import pandas as pd
from ydata_profiling import ProfileReport

# Load your dataset
df = pd.read_csv("my_dataset.csv")

# Generate the profile report
profile = ProfileReport(df, title="Pandas Profiling Report")

# Save to file
profile.to_file("my_report.html")
2. Usage in Jupyter NotebooksIf you are working inside Jupyter (Lab/Notebook), you can render the widgets directly in the cell.# Render the interactive widget
profile.to_widgets()

# OR render as an Iframe (better for GitHub sharing)
profile.to_notebook_iframe()
3. Usage with Large Datasets (Minimal Mode)Generating correlation matrices and deep statistical analysis can be slow on large datasets (>1 million rows). Use minimal=True to disable expensive computations.profile = ProfileReport(df, minimal=True)
profile.to_file("large_dataset_report.html")
⚙️ Advanced ConfigurationYou can control exactly what is calculated by passing a configuration dictionary or loading a YAML configuration file.Example: Disabling Correlations to save timeprofile = ProfileReport(
    df, 
    title="No Correlations Report", 
    correlations={
        "pearson": {"calculate": False},
        "spearman": {"calculate": False},
        "kendall": {"calculate": False},
        "phi_k": {"calculate": False},
        "cramers": {"calculate": False},
    }
)
📊 Command Line UsageYou can also use the library directly from the command line to profile a CSV file without writing a Python script.ydata-profiling my_dataset.csv report.html
🤝 Integrationsydata-profiling integrates seamlessly with other popular data tools:Streamlit: easily embed reports in Streamlit apps using streamlit_pandas_profiling.Dagster/Airflow: Automate data quality checks in your pipelines.Kaggle: Pre-installed in Kaggle kernels for instant EDA.📄 LicenseThis project is licensed under the MIT License.
